IN THE COURT OF THE LEARNED ADDITIONAL DISTRICT JUDGE NO. __, JAIPUR CITY

Civil Suit No. ______ of 2025

Ramesh Kumar Sharma                                                      … Plaintiff

Versus

Suresh Chand Agarwal & Another                                           … Defendants

JUDGMENT
(Draft – Specific Performance Decree Version for Demo)

Date of Decision: _______________

1. This is a suit for specific performance of Agreement to Sell dated 15.01.2024, permanent injunction, and alternative damages.

2. The Plaintiff’s case, in brief, is that he entered into an Agreement to Sell with Defendant No. 1 for a residential plot measuring 200 sq. yards for ₹85,00,000. He paid ₹15,00,000 which was cleared. He remained ready and willing, prepared a Demand Draft for the balance, and was present at the Sub-Registrar’s office on the stipulated date. Defendant No. 1 failed to perform and later sold the property to Defendant No. 2.

3. Defendant No. 1 contested the suit alleging that the Plaintiff was never ready and willing, that time was of the essence, and that the Plaintiff committed default. Defendant No. 2 claimed to be a bona fide purchaser.

4. On the pleadings, the following issues were framed:
   (i) Whether there is a valid Agreement to Sell dated 15.01.2024?
   (ii) Whether the Plaintiff has been ready and willing to perform his part of the contract?
   (iii) Whether time was of the essence and the Plaintiff committed default?
   (iv) Whether Defendant No. 2 is a bona fide purchaser without notice?
   (v) Whether the Plaintiff is entitled to the relief of specific performance and permanent injunction?
   (vi) Relief.

5. Findings:
   Issue (i): Proved. The Agreement is admitted and proved by documentary evidence.
   Issue (ii): Proved. The Plaintiff has established continuous readiness and willingness by payment of earnest money, preparation of Demand Draft, presence at the Sub-Registrar’s office, and issuance of legal notices. The requirements of Section 16(c) of the Specific Relief Act are satisfied.
   Issue (iii): The clause regarding time being essence exists, but the conduct of Defendant No. 1 does not show that he treated time as rigid. No notice of cancellation was issued. The Plaintiff was ready on the stipulated date. This issue is decided against the Defendants.
   Issue (iv): Defendant No. 2 has failed to discharge the burden of proving that he was a bona fide purchaser without notice of the prior Agreement. The issue is decided against Defendant No. 2.
   Issue (v): In view of the findings on Issues (i) to (iv), the Plaintiff is entitled to a decree of specific performance and permanent injunction. The discretion under Section 20 of the Specific Relief Act is exercised in favour of the Plaintiff.

6. Relief:
   The suit is decreed with costs.
   (a) Defendant No. 1 is directed to execute and register the sale deed in favour of the Plaintiff in respect of the suit property described in Schedule-A of the plaint upon the Plaintiff depositing the balance consideration of ₹70,00,000 within 60 days from today. Defendant No. 2 shall join in the execution of the sale deed or suffer the decree.
   (b) The Defendants are permanently restrained from alienating, transferring or creating any third-party interest in the suit property.
   (c) In the event the Plaintiff fails to deposit the balance within the stipulated time, the suit shall stand dismissed.
   (d) Costs of the suit are awarded to the Plaintiff.

Decree-sheet be prepared accordingly.

(Signature)
Additional District Judge
Jaipur City

---
ALTERNATIVE VERSION (Dismissal – for comparative demo)
(If the Court were to hold that continuous readiness was not proved or that time was essence and default occurred, the suit would be dismissed with the observation that the Plaintiff may seek alternative monetary relief in appropriate proceedings. This alternative draft is kept for demonstration of both outcomes.)
